/* ============================================================================
mode-of-data.epher -- the most frequent value, ties and all

The mode of the exam scores in `d` from the builtin mode (guide 1.20):
the value that occurs most often. Two of the ten students scored 85, so
85 is the mode. A second dataset shows the tie rule: when two values
tie for the lead, the builtin reports the smaller one.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/statistics/summary/mode-of-data.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\statistics\summary\mode-of-data.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/statistics/summary/mode-of-data.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

The one knob to edit is `d` below. Everything above the results section
is machinery you can leave alone.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
const d = {72, 85, 91, 85, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}   // exam scores; 85 appears twice
const t = {2, 2, 3, 3, 4}                            // 2 and 3 tie for most frequent

// ---- the mode of the scores ------------------------------------------------------
mode(d)                     // 85 occurs twice, every other score once

// ---- the tie rule: smallest wins -------------------------------------------------
mode(t)                     // 2 and 3 both occur twice; mode reports 2

/* ---- expected output ----
= {72, 85, 91, 85, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}
= {2, 2, 3, 3, 4}
= 85
= 2
*/